  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This preprint has an early but meaningful scholarly footprint: it has 2 citations, has already been reused in downstream scholarship 3 times, and was later published in Optica, indicating that the work moved from preprint to peer-reviewed article.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Phase diversity-based wavefront sensing for fluorescence microscopy addresses a practical bottleneck in adaptive optics for imaging: optical aberrations can reduce contrast, resolution, and signal, limiting what fluorescence microscopes can reveal in biological samples. The paper proposes a wavefront-sensing approach aimed at making aberration correction more usable in routine microscopy settings.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">In the record available here, the strongest evidence of impact is still early-stage scholarly uptake rather than broad public attention. OpenAlex shows 2 citations, with one citation in 2024 and another in 2025. The citing literature spans topics closely aligned with the paper\u2019s core method, including adaptive optics and wavefront sensing, and the citations appear in peer-reviewed articles rather than commentary or informal discussion.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The reuse data also suggests that the work is being incorporated beyond simple citation. Three downstream reuse events are documented, including two articles and one Europe PMC biomedical article mention. That pattern is consistent with methodological relevance: the paper appears to be informing adjacent work in optical measurement, microscopy, and related instrumentation rather than remaining isolated as a standalone preprint.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The publication history further strengthens the story of dissemination. The preprint was later published in Optica, and repository records show open-access availability for both the preprint and the journal version. At the same time, the surrounding attention record is quiet: there are no documented event-based online mentions in the digest, so the evidence here is best read as emerging scholarly use rather than broad community or media reach.</p>",
